{
 "exercise": "adv-88",
 "unit": "unit16-advanced",
 "title": "88: Vtable Hijacking",
 "desc": "Goal: Overwrite the return address by exploiting a vtable hijack scenario. In real C++ exploitation, the attacker overwrites an object's vptr to point to a fake vtable containing the address of win(). Here, a heap object's buffer overflows into the vptr of an adjacent object. The program then calls a virtual method on the corrupted object, which follows the hijacked vptr to your controlled function pointer.Attack vector: The 16-byte buffer buf is followed by the saved EBP and return address on the stack. Overflow the buffer with 16 bytes of padding + 4 bytes for saved EBP + 4 bytes for the address of win(). In a real vtable hijack, you would point the vptr at a fake vtable; here the simulation abstracts this to a return address overwrite that models the same control-flow hijack.",
 "source": [
  "class Base {",
  "public:",
  "    virtual void action() { puts(\"normal\"); }",
  "    char name[8];",
  "};",
  "",
  "void win() { printf(\"FLAG\\n\"); }",
  "",
  "void vuln() {",
  "    char buf[16];  // stack buffer",
  "    Base *obj = new Base();",
  "    gets(buf);     // overflow!",
  "    obj->action(); // virtual call through vptr",
  "}",
  "",
  "// Object layout: [vptr|name[8]]",
  "// vptr -> Base_vtable -> [&action]",
  "// Overwrite vptr -> fake vtable -> win()"
 ],
